AOP in spring boot:
==================
@EnableAspectJAutoProxy is internally present in @springboot Applications, so no need of writing it again.
-> Annotate your aspect as @Aspect.
-> Mark it with @Component annotation. so that the spring will auto detect and register in the spring container.

-> Aspect: The class that encapsulates the cross cutting concerns are said to be the Aspect. In this Aspect we define
the pointcuts and joinpoint().
-> Advise: The Actions that are to be performed at a particular point in the application execution typically called a joinpoint
is said to be advice.
-> pointcut-> its an expression kind a thing by which we determine the join point where the advice should be applied.

-> Advices are few types as below.

-> The actions that your aspect takes at a particular join point is said to be the advice:
1. @Before()-> This Advice is executed before the method execution starts.
2. @After()-> This Advice is executed before the method execution ends.
3. @Around()-> It is used to define the advice in the aspect class that runs before method invocation and after the invocation
as well. It gives you the full controll in the method execution
->You can execute the custom logic prior to the method invocation and after the method invocation as well.
-> The method which is annotared with the @Around annotation must accept the proceeding joinpoint object.
-> This proceeding joinpoint is used to invoke the actual method using proceed() method.
-> Post completion of the execution if the method has any return type will return in the around advise method which we return
to the client.
4. @AfterThrowing("execution(* com.cg.service.saveUser(..))")-> Advice will execute after method completion throwing
an exception.
5. @AfterReturning()->Advice will execute after the method completes its execution normally.

pointcut designators:
1. "execution(* package.*(..)) -> any method within that package with any return type and number of params.
2. "within(pakage.class)-> will intercept all the methods of the class and the aspect is applied.
3. @PointCut("@within(org.stereotype.Service)")-> the class which is anotated with @servic, all of its methods get intercepted.
4. @PointCut("@annotation(org.stereotype.Transactional)")-> The methods which are annotated with @Transactional will be intercepted
and advice will be ran.
5. @pointcut("target(targetobject))whatever the methods were called using the target object wll be intercepted.

✅ JDK Dynamic Proxy

What it is: Built into Java (java.lang.reflect.Proxy).
How it works: Creates a proxy implementing interfaces and delegates calls to an InvocationHandler.
Requirements: Target must implement at least one interface.
Mechanism: Uses reflection to route method calls.
Limitations:

Cannot proxy classes directly.
Only intercepts interface methods.


Spring usage: Default choice if bean implements interfaces.


✅ CGLIB Proxy

What it is: Uses bytecode generation (via ASM) to create a subclass of the target class.
How it works: Overrides methods in the subclass and intercepts calls via MethodInterceptor.
Requirements: Works even if no interfaces exist.
Limitations:

Cannot proxy final classes or final methods.
Slightly heavier than JDK proxy.


Spring usage: Used when bean has no interfaces or when proxyTargetClass = true.

